% GLP System Predicate: Equator (Many-to-One Signaling)
% This enables many-to-one communication patterns where multiple recipients
% can receive and trigger the same equator structure.
% File: stdlib/equator.glp
% Loaded at runtime

%% make_equator(C, Eq, Handle)
%% Creates an equator structure with collapse value C
%%
%% Arguments:
%%   C       - The constant value to bind when equator is triggered (reader)
%%   Eq      - Binds to '_equator'(Handle, C?) - the distributable structure (writer)
%%   Handle  - Fresh variable returned for controller to wait on (writer)
%%
%% Usage:
%%   Controller creates equator, distributes Eq? to multiple workers.
%%   Each worker can call '_equator'(Eq?) to trigger.
%%   Controller waits on Handle? to detect when any worker triggered.
%%
%% Example:
%%   make_equator(stop, Eq, Handle),
%%   distribute(Eq?, Worker1, Worker2, Worker3),
%%   wait_for_trigger(Handle?).
%%
%% The equator structure is: '_equator'(Handle, C)
%% - Handle is a fresh variable (unbound writer)
%% - C is the collapse value (constant)
%% When '_equator'(Eq?) is called, it binds Handle = C, which the controller
%% can detect by waiting on Handle?.
%%
%% The structure is built using unification: Eq = '_equator'(Handle, C?)
make_equator(C, Eq?, Handle?) :-
    Eq = '_equator'(Handle, C?).
